A||N||6||ur2biteme||88 brakes on pre-88s||||5||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||08-28-2000||07:52 AM||||Has anyone attempted to try this?  i'm not at all against the grand am swap, but i want an actual e-brake.  hey, even if all that could be swapped is the rears, that'd be enough.  i'd just do the grand am swap on the front.  any input is appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-28-2000||10:17 AM||||You don't need to do the GA swap on the rear in most applications. You will likely need an additional proportioning valve in that case but it's not a big deal. Remember that rears don't do half the brake loads that the fronts do.<P>MonkeyMan just did the front only. So have a couple others. I'll be doing that method myself. For the same reason, I want the E-brake. If I was you I'd do the front and the prop valve and see how it works for your driving needs before worrying about the rear. (If you look at the rears on most 4 wheel disk sets, even truck sets, you'll see that the rears are far less brakes than the fronts.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||07:38 PM||||Actually, I ended up doing all 4 corners.  I have an automatic so the E-brake wasn't a concern (no inspections in Indiana--heh, heh, heh).  I had originally planned on just doing the fronts, but once I looked at the condition of the parts, I decided that I could upgrade for less than replacements.<P>If you have some machine shop skills, I don't see why you couldn't fab some kind of mount and use one of the aftermarket spot calipers for the E-brake.  It sure wouldn't do much for actual braking, but surely it would hold your car on a hill?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-28-2000||11:15 PM||||those spot calipers are used in some of the conversion kits I've seen for other cars and trucks. Most all of them weigh more than Fiero. They should hold just fine. Likely stop the car better than you'd think. I don't know why no one has made a kit for Fiero. It can't be that hard.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||08-31-2000||02:03 AM||||Thanks for the input.  I'm in the information gathering stages of my resto-mod on my '87 coupe.  i still have a few questions though.  <P>#1  the spot calipers:  are there any prefab units for the fiero (or ones that work with little or no mods)  how much do they cost<BR>#2 if those units don't exhist, how hard are they to build.<BR>#3  i'm also still curious if the 88 rears bolt up or do so with slight mods (i know the hoses are different).  i might still want vented ones in the back later on.  <P>I'm planning on doing a swap to a 3.1 turbo once my funds will allow and would like to have the car ready to handle the performance increase.  any more input would be appreciated.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ur2biteme||09-02-2000||05:40 AM||||anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-02-2000||01:42 PM||||you should be able to find the mechanical caliper.... the problem is one of building the bracket work. No one makes that.||||reg||1
